#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE NINTH STAGE

And I slept, and dreamed again, and saw the same two pilgrims going down the mountains along the highway towards the city. Now a little below these mountains, on the left, lies the country of Conceit; and from that country a little crooked lane comes into the way in which the pilgrims walked. Here, then, they met a very brisk lad who came out of that country, and his name was Ignorance. So Christian asked him what parts he came from, and where he was going.

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Sir, I was born in the country that lies over there, a little to the left, and I am going to the Celestial City.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But how do you expect to get in at the gate? You may find some difficulty there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: As other good people do, he said.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what do you have to show at that gate, so that the gate will be opened to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I know my Lord’s will, and have lived a good life; I pay every man what is his; I pray, fast, pay tithes, and give alms, and I have left my country for the place I am going to.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But you did not come in at the wicket-gate that is at the head of this way; you came in here through that same crooked lane. And so I fear that, whatever you may think of yourself, when the day of reckoning comes, you will have it laid to your charge that you are a thief and a robber, instead of being let into the city.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Gentlemen, you#super[+] are complete strangers to me; I do not know you#super[+]. Be content to follow the religion of your#super[+] country, and I will follow the religion of mine. I hope all will be well. And as for the gate you#super[+] talk of, all the world knows that it is a long way from our country. I cannot think that any man in all our parts so much as knows the way to it; nor do they need to care whether they do or not, since we have, as you#super[+] see, a fine, pleasant, green lane that comes down from our country, the shortest way into the way.]

When Christian saw that the man was wise in his own conceit, he whispered to Hopeful, “There is more hope for a fool than for him.” Prov 26:12. And he went on, “Even as the fool walks along the way, his sense is lacking, and he shows everyone that he is a fool.” Eccl 10:3. What, shall we talk with him further, or go on ahead of him for now, and leave him to think over what he has already heard, and then stop and wait for him later, and see if little by little we can do him any good? Then Hopeful said,

#quote[“Let Ignorance a little while now muse \
On what is said, and let him not refuse \
Good counsel to embrace, lest he remain \
Still ignorant of what’s the chiefest gain. \
God saith, those that no understanding have, \
(Although he made them,) them he will not save.”]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: He added further, I do not think it is good to tell him everything at once; let us pass him by, if you are willing, and talk to him later, as far as he is able to bear it.]

So they both went on, and Ignorance came after them. Now when they had passed him a little way, they entered a very dark lane, where they met a man whom seven devils had bound with seven strong cords, and they were carrying him back to the door that they saw on the side of the hill. Matt 12:45; Prov 5:22. Now good Christian began to tremble, and so did Hopeful, his companion; yet, as the devils led the man away, Christian looked to see if he knew him, and he thought it might be one Turn-away, who lived in the town of Apostasy. But he did not see his face clearly, for the man hung his head like a thief who has been caught. When he had gone past, Hopeful looked after him and saw on his back a paper with this inscription: “Wanton professing Christian, and damnable apostate.”

Then Christian said to his companion, Now I remember something I was told about a thing that happened to a good man around here. The man’s name was Little-Faith, but he was a good man, and he lived in the town of Sincere. This is what happened. At the entrance of this passage, a lane comes down from Broadway-gate called Dead-Man’s lane, so called because of the murders that are commonly done there; and this Little-Faith, going on pilgrimage as we are now, happened to sit down there and sleep. Now at that time three sturdy rogues happened to come down the lane from Broadway-gate, and their names were Faint-Heart, Mistrust, and Guilt, three brothers; and when they spotted Little-Faith where he was, they came galloping up at full speed. Now the good man had just woken from his sleep, and was getting up to go on his journey. So they all came up to him, and with threatening words ordered him to stand still. At this Little-Faith turned as white as a sheet, and had no strength either to fight or to flee. Then Faint-Heart said, Hand over your purse. But as he made no haste to do it (for he was unwilling to lose his money), Mistrust ran up to him, thrust his hand into his pocket, and pulled out a bag of silver. Then Little-Faith cried out, Thieves, thieves! At that, Guilt, with a great club that was in his hand, struck Little-Faith on the head, and with that blow knocked him flat to the ground, where he lay bleeding like one who would bleed to death. All this while the thieves stood by. But at last, hearing that some people were coming along the road, and fearing that it might be one Great-Grace, who lives in the town of Good-Confidence, they took to their heels and left this good man to fend for himself. Now after a while Little-Faith came to himself, and getting up, managed to scramble on his way. That was the story.

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But did they take from him everything he had?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No; they never ransacked the place where his jewels were, so he still kept those. But, as I was told, the good man was much distressed by his loss, for the thieves got most of his spending-money. What they did not get, as I said, were his jewels; he also had a little loose change left, but hardly enough to bring him to his journey’s end. Indeed (if I was not misinformed), he was forced to beg as he went, to keep himself alive, for he was not allowed to sell his jewels; but beg and do what he could, he went, as we say, with many a hungry belly for most of the rest of the way. 1 Pet 4:18.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But is it not a wonder that they did not get his certificate from him, by which he was to be admitted at the Celestial Gate?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It is a wonder; but they did not get that, though it was not through any skill of his that they missed it; for he was so dismayed by their attack that he had neither the strength nor the skill to hide anything. So it was more by good providence than by his own effort that they missed that good thing. 2 Tim 1:12–14; 2 Pet 2:9.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But it must surely be a comfort to him that they did not get this jewel from him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It might have been a great comfort to him, had he used it as he should; but those who told me the story said that he made very little use of it all the rest of the way, and that because of the dismay he felt at their taking away his money. In fact, he forgot it for a great part of the rest of his journey; and besides, whenever it came into his mind and he began to be comforted by it, fresh thoughts of his loss would come over him again, and these thoughts would swallow up everything.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Alas, poor man, this must have been a great grief to him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Grief? Yes, a grief indeed! Would it not have been so to any of us, had we been treated as he was, robbed and wounded too, and that in a strange place, as he was? It is a wonder he did not die of grief, poor soul. I was told that he scattered almost all the rest of the way with nothing but sad and bitter complaints; telling everyone who overtook him, or whom he overtook on the way as he went, where he was robbed, and how; who it was that did it, and what he had lost; how he was wounded, and that he barely escaped with his life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But it is a wonder that his need did not drive him to sell or pawn some of his jewels, so that he might have something to sustain himself with on his journey.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You talk like one who still has the shell on his head to this very day. Why should he pawn them? Or to whom should he sell them? In all that country where he was robbed, his jewels were thought nothing of; nor did he need the relief that could be given him from there. Besides, had his jewels been missing at the gate of the Celestial City, he would have been (and he knew this well enough) shut out from an inheritance there, and that would have been worse to him than the appearance and villainy of ten thousand thieves.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why are you so sharp, my brother? Esau sold his birthright, and that for a mess of pottage, Heb 12:16; and that birthright was his greatest jewel. And if he did, why might not Little-Faith do so too?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Esau did indeed sell his birthright, and so do many others, and by doing so they shut themselves out from the chief blessing, as that wretch did too. But you must make a distinction between Esau and Little-Faith, and also between their states. Esau’s birthright was a type; but Little-Faith’s jewels were not. Esau’s belly was his god; but Little-Faith’s belly was not. Esau’s need lay in his fleshly appetite; Little-Faith’s did not. Besides, Esau could see no further than the fulfilling of his lusts: For I am about to die, he said, and what good will this birthright do me? Gen 25:32. But Little-Faith, though it was his lot to have only a little faith, was kept by his little faith from such extravagances, and made to see and prize his jewels too much to sell them, as Esau did his birthright. You do not read anywhere that Esau had faith, no, not even a little; so it is no wonder, where the flesh alone holds sway (as it will in the man who has no faith to resist it), if he sells his birthright and his soul and all, and that to the devil of hell; for it is with such men as it is with the donkey, who in her heat cannot be turned away, Jer 2:24: when their minds are set on their lusts, they will have them, whatever they cost. But Little-Faith was of another disposition; his mind was on things divine; his livelihood was in things that were spiritual, and from above. So to what end should a man of such a disposition sell his jewels (had there been anyone who would have bought them) to fill his mind with empty things? Will a man give a penny to fill his belly with hay? Or can you persuade the turtle-dove to live on carrion, like the crow? Though faithless people can, for carnal lusts, pawn, or mortgage, or sell what they have, and themselves outright into the bargain, yet those who have faith, saving faith, though only a little of it, cannot do so. Here, then, my brother, is your mistake.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I admit it; but still, your severe rebuke had almost made me angry.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, I only compared you to some of the birds of the brisker sort, which will run to and fro on untrodden paths with the shell still on their heads. But let that pass, and consider the matter we are discussing, and all will be well between you and me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But, Christian, I am persuaded in my heart that these three fellows are nothing but a pack of cowards. Would they otherwise have run as they did, do you think, at the noise of someone coming along the road? Why did Little-Faith not pluck up a greater heart? I think he might have stood one brush with them, and given in when there was no help for it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That they are cowards many have said, but few have found it so in the time of trial. As for a great heart, Little-Faith had none; and I can see from what you say, my brother, that had you been the man concerned, you would stand only one brush, and then give in. And truly, since this is how high your courage runs now that they are far away from us, should they appear to you as they did to him, they might give you second thoughts.]

But consider again that they are only journeymen thieves; they serve under the king of the bottomless pit, who, if need be, will come to their aid himself, and his voice is like the roaring of a lion. 1 Pet 5:8. I myself have been attacked as this Little-Faith was, and I found it a terrible thing. These three villains set on me, and as I began like a Christian to resist, they gave just one call, and in came their master. I would, as the saying goes, have given my life for a penny, but, as God would have it, I was clothed in tested armor. Yes, and even so, though I was armed like that, I found it hard work to acquit myself like a man. No man can tell what we face in that combat but he who has been in the battle himself.

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Well, but they ran, you see, when they merely supposed that one Great-Grace was on the road.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: True, they have often fled, both they and their master, when Great-Grace has merely appeared; and no wonder, for he is the King’s champion. But I suppose you will make some distinction between Little-Faith and the King’s champion. Not all the King’s subjects are his champions; nor can they, when tested, do such feats of war as he does. Is it right to think that a little child could handle Goliath as David did? Or that there should be the strength of an ox in a wren? Some are strong, some are weak; some have great faith, some have little. This man was one of the weak, and so he went to the wall.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I wish it had been Great-Grace, for their sakes.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: If it had been he, he might have had his hands full. For I must tell you that, though Great-Grace is excellent with his weapons, and has done and can do well enough with them so long as he keeps them at sword’s point, yet if they get within his guard, even Faint-Heart, Mistrust, or the other, they will almost certainly knock him off his feet. And when a man is down, you know, what can he do?]

Whoever looks closely at Great-Grace’s face will see scars and cuts there that will easily prove what I say. Indeed, I once heard that he said (and that when he was in the combat), We despaired even of life. How these sturdy rogues and their fellows made David groan, mourn, and roar! Yes, Heman, Ps 88, and Hezekiah too, though champions in their day, were forced to rouse themselves when these attacked them; and even so, they had their coats soundly dusted by them. Peter once wanted to go and try what he could do; but though some say of him that he is the prince of the apostles, they handled him so that in the end they made him afraid of a mere girl.

Besides, their king comes at their whistle; he is never out of hearing; and if at any time they are getting the worst of it, he comes in to help them if he can; and of him it is said, “The sword that reaches him has no effect, nor does the spear or dart or arrow. He regards iron as straw and bronze as rotten wood. No arrow can make him flee; slingstones become like chaff to him. A club is regarded as straw, and he laughs at the sound of the lance.” Job 41:26–29. What can a man do in such a case? It is true, if a man could have Job’s horse at every turn, and had the skill and courage to ride him, he might do notable things. “For his neck is adorned with a mane; he leaps like a locust, striking terror with his proud snorting. He paws in the valley and rejoices in his strength; he charges into battle. He laughs at fear, frightened of nothing; he does not turn back from the sword. A quiver rattles at his side, along with a flashing spear and lance. Trembling with excitement, he devours the distance; he cannot stand still when the ram’s horn sounds. At the blast of the horn, he snorts with fervor. He catches the scent of battle from afar—the shouts of captains and the cry of war.” Job 39:19–25.

But as for foot soldiers such as you and I are, let us never wish to meet an enemy, nor boast as if we could do better when we hear of others who have been beaten, nor be tickled by the thought of our own manhood; for such people commonly come off worst when tested. Witness Peter, whom I mentioned before: he would swagger, yes, he would; he would, as his vain mind prompted him to say, do better and stand up more for his Master than all men; but who was so beaten and run down by those villains as he?

So when we hear that such robberies are done on the King’s highway, there are two things we ought to do.

1\. To go out armed, and be sure to take a shield with us; for it was for lack of a shield that he who laid on so vigorously at Leviathan could not make him yield; for indeed, if that is lacking, he does not fear us at all. That is why he who had skill in this has said, “Above all, take up the shield of faith, with which you#super[+] can quench all the fiery darts of the evil one.” Eph 6:16.

2\. It is good also that we ask the King for an escort, yes, that he will go with us himself. This made David rejoice when he was in the Valley of the Shadow of Death; and Moses would rather have died where he stood than go one step without his God. Exod 33:15.

O my brother, if he will only go along with us, why should we be afraid of tens of thousands who set themselves against us? Ps 3:5–8; 27:1–3. But without him, the proud helpers fall under the slain. Isa 10:4.

For my part, I have been in the fray before now; and though (through the goodness of Him who is best) I am alive, as you see, yet I cannot boast of any manhood. I will be glad if I meet no more such onslaughts; though I fear we are not yet past all danger. However, since the lion and the bear have not yet devoured me, I hope God will also deliver us from the next uncircumcised Philistine. Then Christian sang,

#quote[“Poor Little-Faith! hast been among the thieves? \
Wast robb’d? Remember this, whoso believes, \
And get more faith; then shall you victors be \
Over ten thousand—else scarce over three.”]

So they went on, and Ignorance followed. They went on until they came to a place where they saw another way join their way, and it seemed to lie as straight as the way they ought to go; and here they did not know which of the two to take, for both seemed straight before them. So here they stood still to think it over. And as they were thinking about the way, a man black of flesh, but covered with a very light robe, came up to them and asked them why they were standing there. They answered that they were going to the Celestial City, but did not know which of these ways to take. “Follow me,” said the man, “that is where I am going.” So they followed him in the way that had just come into the road, which little by little turned, and turned them so far from the city they wanted to go to that in a short time their faces were turned away from it; yet they followed him. But soon, before they were aware of it, he led them both into the middle of a net, in which they were both so entangled that they did not know what to do; and with that the white robe fell off the black man’s back. Then they saw where they were. So there they lay crying for some time, for they could not get themselves out.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said to his companion, Now I see that I was mistaken. Did the shepherds not tell us to beware of the Flatterer? As the wise man says, so we have found it today: “A man who flatters his neighbor spreads a net for his feet.” Prov 29:5.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: They also gave us a note of directions about the way, so that we might find it more surely; but we have forgotten to read that too, and have not kept ourselves from the paths of the destroyer. Here David was wiser than we are; for he says, “As for the deeds of men, by the word of Your lips I have kept myself from the paths of the destroyer.” Ps 17:4. So they lay in the net, lamenting their plight. At last they saw a Shining One coming towards them with a whip of small cords in his hand. When he had come to the place where they were, he asked them where they came from, and what they were doing there. They told him that they were poor pilgrims going to Zion, but had been led out of their way by a black man clothed in white, who told us, they said, to follow him, for he was going there too. Then the one with the whip said, It is Flatterer, a false apostle, who has transformed himself into an angel of light. Dan 11:32; 2 Cor 11:13, 14. So he tore the net and let the men out. Then he said to them, Follow me, so that I may set you on your way again. So he led them back to the way they had left to follow the Flatterer. Then he asked them, Where did you sleep last night? They said, With the shepherds on the Delectable Mountains. He then asked them whether the shepherds had not given them a note of directions for the way. They answered, Yes. But when you were at a standstill, he said, did you not take out your note and read it? They answered, No. He asked them, Why? They said they forgot. He asked, moreover, whether the shepherds had not told them to beware of the Flatterer. They answered, Yes; but we did not imagine, they said, that this fine-spoken man was he. Rom 16:17, 18.]

Then I saw in my dream that he commanded them to lie down; and when they did, he chastised them severely, to teach them the good way in which they should walk, Deut 25:2; 2 Chr 6:27; and as he chastised them, he said, “Those I love, I rebuke and chasten. Therefore be earnest and repent.” Rev 3:19. When this was done, he told them to go on their way, and to pay close attention to the shepherds’ other directions. So they thanked him for all his kindness, and went quietly along the right way, singing,

#quote[“Come hither, you that walk along the way, \
See how the pilgrims fare that go astray: \
They catched are in an entangling net, \
Cause they good counsel lightly did forget: \
’Tis true, they rescued were; but yet, you see, \
They’re scourged to boot; let this your caution be.”]

Now after a while they saw someone far off, coming quietly and alone along the highway to meet them. Then Christian said to his companion, There is a man over there with his back towards Zion, and he is coming to meet us.

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I see him; let us be on our guard now, in case he turns out to be a Flatterer too. So he drew nearer and nearer, and at last came up to them. His name was Atheist, and he asked them where they were going.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: We are going to Mount Zion.]

Then Atheist burst into a great fit of laughter.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why are you laughing?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: I laugh to see what ignorant people you are, to take on so tedious a journey, when you are likely to get nothing but your travel for your pains.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, man, do you think we will not be received?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: Received! There is no such place as you dream of in all this world.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But there is in the world to come.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: When I was at home in my own country I heard what you now affirm, and having heard it I went out to see, and I have been looking for this city for twenty years, but I find no more of it than I did the first day I set out. Eccl 10:15; Jer 17:15.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: We have both heard, and we believe, that there is such a place to be found.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: If I had not believed, when I was at home, I would not have come this far to look; but finding none (and yet I would have found it, had there been such a place to be found, for I have gone farther than you to look for it), I am going back again, and will try to refresh myself with the things that I then threw away in hope of what I now see does not exist.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said to Hopeful his companion, Is what this man has said true?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Be careful; he is one of the Flatterers. Remember what it has already cost us once to listen to fellows of that kind. What! No Mount Zion? Did we not see the gate of the city from the Delectable Mountains? Also, are we not now to walk by faith? 2 Cor 5:7.]

Let us go on, in case the man with the whip overtakes us again. You should have taught me the lesson that I will now sound in your ears: “Cease, my son, to hear the instruction that causes you to stray from the words of knowledge.” Prov 19:27. I say, my brother, cease to hear him, and let us believe to the saving of the soul.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: My brother, I did not put the question to you because I doubted the truth of our belief myself, but to test you, and to draw from you a fruit of the honesty of your heart. As for this man, I know that he is blinded by the god of this world. Let us go on, you and I, knowing that we have belief in the truth, and no lie is of the truth. 1 John 2:21.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Now I rejoice in hope of the glory of God. So they turned away from the man; and he, laughing at them, went his way.]

I then saw in my dream that they went on until they came into a certain country whose air naturally tended to make one drowsy, if he came into it as a stranger. And here Hopeful began to feel very dull and heavy with sleep; so he said to Christian, I am now growing so drowsy that I can hardly keep my eyes open; let us lie down here and take one nap.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: By no means, the other said; for if we sleep, we may never wake again.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why, my brother? Sleep is sweet to the laboring man; we may be refreshed if we take a nap.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Don’t you remember that one of the shepherds told us to beware of the Enchanted Ground? He meant by that that we should beware of sleeping; so “let us not sleep as the others do, but let us remain awake and sober.” 1 Thess 5:6.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I admit I was at fault; and had I been here alone, I would have run the risk of death by sleeping. I see that what the wise man says is true: “Two are better than one.” Eccl 4:9. Until now your company has been a mercy to me; and you will have a good reward for your labor.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Now, then, Christian said, to keep off drowsiness in this place, let us fall into some good conversation.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: With all my heart, the other said.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Where shall we begin?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Where God began with us. But you begin, if you would.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: First I will sing you this song:]

#quote[“When saints do sleepy grow, let them come hither, \
And hear how these two pilgrims talk together; \
Yea, let them learn of them in any wise, \
Thus to keep open their drowsy, slumb’ring eyes. \
Saints’ fellowship, if it be managed well, \
Keeps them awake, and that in spite of hell.”]

Then Christian began, and said, I will ask you a question. How did you first come to think of doing what you are doing now?

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Do you mean, how did I first come to seek the good of my soul?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, that is what I mean.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: For a long time I went on delighting in the things that were seen and sold at our fair; things which, I now believe, would have drowned me in perdition and destruction, had I gone on in them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What things were they?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: All the treasures and riches of the world. I also took great delight in carousing, reveling, drinking, swearing, lying, uncleanness, Sabbath-breaking, and everything else that tended to destroy the soul. But at last I found, by hearing and thinking about divine things, which indeed I heard from you, and also from beloved Faithful, who was put to death for his faith and good living in Vanity Fair, that the end of these things is death, Rom 6:21–23; and that because of these things the wrath of God comes on the children of disobedience. Eph 5:6.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you at once fall under the power of this conviction?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: No, I was not willing to know at once the evil of sin, or the damnation that follows on committing it; but when my mind first began to be shaken by the word, I tried to shut my eyes against its light.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what was the reason you treated the first workings of God’s blessed Spirit on you like this?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: The causes were: 1. I did not know that this was the work of God on me. I never thought that God begins the conversion of a sinner by first awakening him to his sin. 2. Sin was still very sweet to my flesh, and I was unwilling to leave it. 3. I did not know how to part with my old companions; their company and doings were so pleasing to me. 4. The hours when convictions of sin were on me were such troubling and such terrifying hours that I could not bear, no, not even the memory of them on my heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then, as it seems, at times you got rid of your trouble?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, truly, but it would come back into my mind; and then I would be as bad as I was before, indeed worse.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what was it that brought your sins to mind again?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Many things, such as:]

1\. If I merely met a good man in the street; or,

2\. If I heard anyone read from the Bible; or,

3\. If my head began to ache; or,

4\. If I was told that some of my neighbors were sick; or,

5\. If I heard the bell toll for any who had died; or,

6\. If I thought of dying myself; or,

7\. If I heard that sudden death had come to others.

8\. But especially when I thought of myself, that I must soon come to judgment.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And could you ever easily get rid of the guilt of sin, when it came on you in any of these ways?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: No, not I; for then they got a tighter hold on my conscience; and then, if I so much as thought of going back to sin (though my mind was turned against it), it would be a double torment to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I thought I must try to mend my life; for otherwise, I thought, I am sure to be damned.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you try to mend it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, and I fled not only from my sins but from sinful company too, and took up religious duties, such as praying, reading, weeping for sin, speaking the truth to my neighbors, etc. These things I did, with many others, too many to tell here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you think all was well with you then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, for a while; but in the end my trouble came tumbling down on me again, and that in spite of all my reformations.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: How did that come about, since you were now reformed?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: There were several things that brought it on me, especially such sayings as these: “All our righteousnesses are like filthy rags.” Isa 64:6. “By the works of the law no flesh will be justified.” Gal 2:16. “When you#super[+] have done all these things, say, ‘We are unprofitable,’” Luke 17:10; with many more like them. From these I began to reason with myself like this: If all my righteousnesses are like filthy rags; if by the deeds of the law no man can be justified; and if, when we have done all, we are still unprofitable, then it is mere folly to think of heaven by the law. I also thought this: If a man runs up a debt of a hundred pounds with the shopkeeper, and after that pays for everything he buys; yet if his old debt still stands in the book, not crossed out, the shopkeeper may sue him for it and throw him into prison until he pays the debt.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, and how did you apply this to yourself?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why, I thought like this to myself: I have run a long way into God’s book by my sins, and my reforming now will not pay off that debt; so I should still be thinking, for all my present improvements, But how shall I be freed from the damnation I brought myself in danger of by my former transgressions?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: A very good application; but please go on.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Another thing that has troubled me ever since my recent improvements is that if I look closely at the best of what I do now, I still see sin, new sin, mixing itself with the best of what I do. So now I am forced to conclude that, in spite of my former foolish fancies about myself and my duties, I have committed enough sin in one day to send me to hell, even if my former life had been faultless.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Do! I did not know what to do, until I opened my mind to Faithful, for he and I knew each other well. And he told me that unless I could obtain the righteousness of a man who had never sinned, neither my own righteousness nor all the righteousness of the world could save me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you think he was telling the truth?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: If he had told me that when I was pleased and satisfied with my own improvements, I would have called him a fool for his pains; but now, since I see my own weakness, and the sin that clings to my best performance, I have been forced to agree with him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But when he first suggested it to you, did you think that such a man could be found, of whom it could rightly be said that he never committed sin?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I must confess the words sounded strange at first; but after a little more talk and time with him, I was fully convinced of it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you ask him who this man was, and how you must be justified by him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, and he told me it was the Lord Jesus, who dwells at the right hand of the Most High. Heb 10:12–21. And this, he said, is how you must be justified by him: by trusting in what he has done by himself in the days of his flesh, and suffered when he hung on the tree. Rom 4:5; Col 1:14; 1 Pet 1:19. I asked him further how that man’s righteousness could have such efficacy as to justify another before God. And he told me he was the mighty God, and did what he did, and died the death too, not for himself but for me; and that his doings, and their worthiness, would be imputed to me if I believed in him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I raised objections against believing, because I thought he was not willing to save me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did Faithful say to you then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: He told me to go to him and see. Then I said it would be presumption. He said, No, for I was invited to come. Matt 11:28. Then he gave me a book of Jesus’ composing, to encourage me to come more freely; and he said of that book that every jot and tittle of it stood firmer than heaven and earth. Matt 24:35. Then I asked him what I must do when I came; and he told me I must plead on my knees, Ps 95:6; Dan 6:10, with all my heart and soul, Jer 29:12, 13, asking the Father to reveal him to me. Then I asked him further how I must make my prayers to him; and he said, Go, and you will find him on a mercy-seat, where he sits all year round to give pardon and forgiveness to those who come. Exod 25:22; Lev 16:2; Num 7:89; Heb 4:16. I told him that I did not know what to say when I came; and he told me to say something like this: God be merciful to me a sinner, and make me know and believe in Jesus Christ; for I see that if it were not for his righteousness, or if I do not have faith in that righteousness, I am utterly cast away. Lord, I have heard that you are a merciful God, and have ordained that your Son Jesus Christ should be the Savior of the world; and moreover, that you are willing to give him to such a poor sinner as I am—and I am a sinner indeed. Lord, take this opportunity, then, and magnify your grace in the salvation of my soul, through your Son Jesus Christ. Amen.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you do as you were told?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, over, and over, and over.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did the Father reveal the Son to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Not the first time, nor the second, nor the third, nor the fourth, nor the fifth; no, nor the sixth time either.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: What? Why, I did not know what to do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you not think of giving up praying?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes; a hundred times twice over.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what was the reason you did not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I believed that what I had been told was true, namely, that without the righteousness of this Christ all the world could not save me; and so I thought to myself, If I give up, I die, and I can only die at the throne of grace. At the same time, this came into my mind: “Though it lingers, wait for it, since it will surely come and will not delay.” Hab 2:3. So I went on praying until the Father showed me his Son.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And how was he revealed to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I did not see him with my bodily eyes, but with the eyes of my understanding, Eph 1:18, 19; and this is how it was. One day I was very sad, sadder, I think, than at any other time in my life; and this sadness came from a fresh sight of the greatness and vileness of my sins. And as I was then expecting nothing but hell, and the everlasting damnation of my soul, suddenly, as I thought, I saw the Lord Jesus looking down on me from heaven, and saying, “Believe in the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved.” Acts 16:31.]

But I replied, Lord, I am a great, a very great sinner; and he answered, “My grace is sufficient for you.” 2 Cor 12:9. Then I said, But, Lord, what is believing? And then I saw from that saying, “Whoever comes to Me will never hunger, and whoever believes in Me will never thirst,” John 6:35, that believing and coming were one and the same; and that he who came, that is, whose heart and affections ran out after salvation by Christ, did indeed believe in Christ. Then tears stood in my eyes, and I asked further, But, Lord, may such a great sinner as I am really be accepted by you, and be saved by you? And I heard him say, “And him who comes to Me I will by no means cast out.” John 6:37. Then I said, But how, Lord, must I think of you in my coming to you, so that my faith may rest rightly on you? Then he said, “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners.” 1 Tim 1:15. He is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes. Rom 10:4, and ch. 4. He died for our sins, and rose again for our justification. Rom 4:25. He loved us, and washed us from our sins in his own blood. Rev 1:5. He is the Mediator between God and us. 1 Tim 2:5. He always lives to make intercession for us. Heb 7:25. From all this I gathered that I must look for righteousness in his person, and for satisfaction for my sins by his blood; that what he did in obedience to his Father’s law, and in submitting to its penalty, was not for himself, but for him who will accept it for his salvation, and be thankful. And now my heart was full of joy, my eyes full of tears, and my affections running over with love to the name, people, and ways of Jesus Christ.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: This was a revelation of Christ to your soul indeed. But tell me in particular what effect this had on your spirit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: It made me see that all the world, for all its righteousness, is in a state of condemnation. It made me see that God the Father, though he is just, can justly justify the sinner who comes. It made me deeply ashamed of the vileness of my former life, and confounded me with a sense of my own ignorance; for never before had a thought come into my heart that showed me the beauty of Jesus Christ like that. It made me love a holy life, and long to do something for the honor and glory of the name of the Lord Jesus. Yes, I thought that if I now had a thousand gallons of blood in my body, I could spill it all for the sake of the Lord Jesus.]

I saw then in my dream that Hopeful looked back and saw Ignorance, whom they had left behind, coming after them. Look, he said to Christian, how far behind that youngster over there is lagging.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, yes, I see him; he does not care for our company.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But I suppose it would not have hurt him to keep pace with us so far.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That is true; but I am sure he thinks otherwise.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I think he does; but all the same, let us wait for him. (So they did.)]

Then Christian said to him, Come along, man; why do you lag so far behind?

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I enjoy walking alone, a great deal more than walking in company, unless I like the company better.]

Then Christian said to Hopeful (but quietly), Did I not tell you he did not care for our company? But all the same, he said, come up, and let us talk away the time in this lonely place. Then, turning to Ignorance, he said, Come, how are you? How do things stand between God and your soul now?

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Well, I hope; for I am always full of good stirrings that come into my mind to comfort me as I walk.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What good stirrings? Please tell us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Why, I think of God and heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: So do the devils and damned souls.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But I think of them, and desire them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: So do many who are never likely to get there. “The soul of the sluggard desires, and has nothing.” Prov 13:4.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But I think of them, and leave all for them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That I doubt; for to leave all is a very hard thing; indeed, a harder thing than many realize. But why, or by what, are you persuaded that you have left all for God and heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: My heart tells me so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: The wise man says, “He who trusts in his own heart is a fool.” Prov 28:26.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: That is spoken of an evil heart; but mine is a good one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But how do you prove that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: It comforts me with hopes of heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That may be through its deceitfulness; for a man’s heart may give him comfort in hoping for a thing he has as yet no ground to hope for.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But my heart and life agree; and so my hope is well-grounded.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Who told you that your heart and life agree?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: My heart tells me so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: “Ask my fellow if I am a thief.” Your heart tells you so! Unless the word of God bears witness in this matter, no other testimony is of any value.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But is it not a good heart that has good thoughts? And is it not a good life that is lived according to God’s commandments?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, that is a good heart that has good thoughts, and that is a good life that is lived according to God’s commandments; but it is one thing really to have these, and another only to think so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Tell me, what do you count as good thoughts, and a life according to God’s commandments?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: There are good thoughts of various kinds: some concerning ourselves, some God, some Christ, and some other things.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What are good thoughts concerning ourselves?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Such as agree with the word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: When do our thoughts of ourselves agree with the word of God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: When we pass the same judgment on ourselves that the word passes. To explain myself: the word of God says of people in their natural condition, “There is no one righteous, there is no one who does good.” It says also that “every inclination of the thoughts of man’s heart is altogether evil all the time.” Gen 6:5; Rom 3. And again, “Every inclination of man’s heart is evil from his youth.” Gen 8:21. Now, then, when we think this of ourselves, and feel it to be so, then our thoughts are good ones, because they agree with the word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I will never believe that my heart is as bad as that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then you have never had one good thought about yourself in your life. But let me go on. As the word passes a judgment on our hearts, so it passes a judgment on our ways; and when the thoughts of our hearts and ways agree with the judgment that the word gives of both, then both are good, because they agree with it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Explain what you mean.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, the word of God says that man’s ways are crooked ways, not good but perverse; it says that they are by nature out of the good way, that they have not known it. Ps 125:5; Prov 2:15; Rom 3:12. Now, when a man thinks this of his ways, I say, when he thinks it with feeling and with a humbled heart, then he has good thoughts of his own ways, because his thoughts now agree with the judgment of the word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What are good thoughts concerning God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Just as I have said concerning ourselves: when our thoughts of God agree with what the word says of him; and that is, when we think of his being and attributes as the word has taught, of which I cannot now speak more fully. But to speak of him in relation to us: we have right thoughts of God when we think that he knows us better than we know ourselves, and can see sin in us when and where we can see none in ourselves; when we think he knows our inmost thoughts, and that our heart, with all its depths, is always open to his eyes; also when we think that all our righteousness stinks in his nostrils, and that therefore he cannot bear to see us stand before him in any confidence, even in all our best performances.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Do you think I am such a fool as to think that God can see no further than I can, or that I would come to God in the best of my performances?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what do you think in this matter?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Why, to be short, I think I must believe in Christ for justification.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What! You think you must believe in Christ, when you do not see your need of him! You see neither your original nor your actual infirmities, but have such an opinion of yourself, and of what you do, that it plainly shows you to be one who never saw the necessity of Christ’s personal righteousness to justify you before God. How, then, can you say, I believe in Christ?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I believe well enough, for all that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: How do you believe?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I believe that Christ died for sinners, and that I will be justified before God from the curse through his gracious acceptance of my obedience to his laws. Or put it this way: Christ makes my religious duties acceptable to his Father by virtue of his merits, and so I will be justified.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Let me give an answer to this confession of your faith.]

1\. You believe with a fanciful faith; for this faith is nowhere described in the word.

2\. You believe with a false faith; because it takes justification from the personal righteousness of Christ and applies it to your own.

3\. This faith makes Christ a justifier not of your person but of your actions, and of your person for your actions’ sake, which is false.

4\. So this faith is deceitful, the kind that will leave you under wrath in the day of God Almighty. For true justifying faith, when the soul is made aware of its lost condition by the law, sets it fleeing for refuge to Christ’s righteousness (and this righteousness of his is not an act of grace by which he makes your obedience accepted with God for justification, but his personal obedience to the law, in doing and suffering for us what the law required of us). This righteousness, I say, true faith accepts; and the soul, covered under the fold of its robe, and presented by it as spotless before God, is accepted, and acquitted from condemnation.

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What! Would you have us trust in what Christ has done in his own person without us? This notion would loosen the reins of our lust, and let us live as we please; for what does it matter how we live, if we may be justified from all by Christ’s personal righteousness, when we believe it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Ignorance is your name, and as your name is, so are you: this very answer of yours proves what I say. You are ignorant of what justifying righteousness is, and just as ignorant of how to secure your soul from the heavy wrath of God through faith in it. Yes, you are also ignorant of the true effects of saving faith in this righteousness of Christ, which are to bow and win over the heart to God in Christ, to love his name, his word, ways, and people, and not as you ignorantly imagine.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Ask him whether he ever had Christ revealed to him from heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What! You are a man for revelations! I do believe that what you and all the rest of you say about that matter is nothing but the fruit of crazed brains.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why, man, Christ is so hidden in God from the natural understanding of the flesh that he cannot be savingly known by any man, unless God the Father reveals him to him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: That is your faith, but not mine; yet mine, I have no doubt, is as good as yours, though I do not have as many whims in my head as you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Allow me to put in a word. You ought not to speak so lightly of this matter; for I will boldly affirm this, as my good companion has done, that no man can know Jesus Christ except by the revelation of the Father; yes, and faith too, by which the soul lays hold of Christ (if it is right), must be worked by the exceeding greatness of his mighty power, Matt 11:27; 1 Cor 12:3; Eph 1:17–19; and of the working of that faith, I see, poor Ignorance, you are ignorant. Wake up, then, see your own wretchedness, and flee to the Lord Jesus; and by his righteousness, which is the righteousness of God (for he himself is God), you will be delivered from condemnation.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: You go so fast I cannot keep pace with you; you go on ahead: I must stay behind a while.]

Then they said,

#quote[“Well, Ignorance, wilt thou yet foolish be, \
To slight good counsel, ten times given thee? \
And if thou yet refuse it, thou shalt know, \
Ere long, the evil of thy doing so. \
Remember, man, in time: stoop, do not fear: \
Good counsel, taken well, saves; therefore hear. \
But if thou yet shalt slight it, thou wilt be \
The loser, Ignorance, I’ll warrant thee.”]
